(a) It shall be unlawful for any operator, directly or indirectly, or by any servant, agent or employee to:

(1) Use any flammable stage or movable equipment without first obtaining the approval of the fire department;

(2) Permit the creation of a public nuisance through the emanation of loud music into parking areas and the immediate neighborhood so as to disturb the public peace;

(3) Permit illegal activities on the premises;

(4) Permit smoking in the area designated for dancing;

(5) Permit overcrowding in the area designated for dancing;

(6) Employ a person who is under the age of 18; nor shall any person under 18 be allowed to participate in any talent show, audition for any talent show or provide any entertainment. The provisions of this subsection shall not be applicable to teen clubs;

(7) Permit any person under the age of 18 to be upon the premises, unless accompanied by a parent or legal guardian, except where other sections of this article expressly allow persons under 18 to be on the premises.

(b) A copy of the acts prohibited under subsection (a) of this section shall be issued with all public dance hall permits and shall be displayed next to the permit.

(Code 1956, § 63-26.7)

No person may sponsor, promote, or train an animal to participate in, or contribute to the involvement of an animal in, or attend as a spectator, any event or activity in which an animal:

(1) Engages in unnatural or generally-repugnant behavior;

(2) Is wrestled or fought (such as bear wrestling, cockfighting and dog fighting);

(3) Is mentally or physically harassed;

(4) Is displayed in such a way that the animal is abused or stressed mentally or physically; or

(5) Is induced or encouraged to perform through the use of chemical, mechanical, electrical or manual devices in a manner that will cause or is likely to cause physical injury or suffering.

(Code 1956, § 7-17(d); M.C.S., Ord. No. 25205, § 1, 3-7-13)

No person may own, keep, or harbor any dog within the city that has not been spayed or neutered unless:

(a) The dog is under six months of age;

(b) A licensed veterinarian certifies that the dog should not be spayed or neutered for a specific reason. The certificate must list the veterinarian’s license number and the date by which the dog can be spayed or neutered. Within 30 days of the effective date of this division, or within 30 days of taking possession or ownership of an intact dog, the owner or keeper must submit written documentation which can be verified by the agency and the owner or keeper must apply for and obtain an intact dog permit.

(c) The dog has been present in the city for less than 30 days;

(d) The owner or keeper has obtained, or has submitted, an application for an intact dog permit in accordance with the provisions of this chapter;

(e) The dog is used by a government or law enforcement agency or an agent thereof for government, search and rescue, law enforcement, or other government purposes.

(M.C.S., Ord. No. 23970, § 1, 5-6-10; M.C.S., Ord. No. 25205, § 1, 3-7-13)

(a) Any demolition of a structure within National Register Historic Districts, not expressly exempt by section 26-6(f), shall be reviewed and approved by the New Orleans City Council at a public hearing.

(b) Applications may only be filed by the owner of the subject property, an authorized agent of such owner, or a governmental agency. All applications subject to review, excluding applications that are initiated by a government agency, shall be accompanied by an additional application review fee of $250.00 per residential property and $500.00 per commercial property. This fee shall be in addition to any fees and applications required by the department of safety and permits for a standard demolition permit. For city initiated demolitions, all application submittals are required.

(c) To initiate review, the applicant shall submit to the department of safety and permits a completed application on forms created by the department for this purpose, which shall require at a minimum:

(1) The name of the owner(s);

(2) The name of the applicant(s), if different than the owner;

(3) Signature of the owner(s), or proof that the agent filing on behalf of an owner is authorized to do so;

(4) If the applicant is not the owner, the relationship to the owner (lessee, contractor, architect, etc.);

(5) The contact information for the applicant (phone number, address, email, etc.);

(6) The municipal address of the property;

(7) The following information regarding the property:

i. The Council district where the property is located;

ii. If the property is located in a National Register Historic District;

iii. If the property is in a Historic District Landmarks Commission District; and

iv. If the property is in the Vieux Carré (French Quarter).

(8) The approximate length of time the owner(s) owned the property;

(9) A written statement regarding why demolition is being sought;

(10) The cost of the demolition;

(11) A written statement regarding the future development plans for the property, specifically including:

i. The proposed plan for maintaining the lot between demolition and redevelopment, including any lot/weed maintenance and trash/debris removal plans;

ii. If fencing is proposed on the perimeter of the lot after demolition, the kind of fencing proposed;

iii. If redevelopment is planned, the timing expectations for completing the development along with the proposed use and if the redevelopment meets zoning requirements (if known); and

iv. If no immediate redevelopment is planned, the length of time the lot is anticipated to remain vacant.

(12) The number of residential units to be demolished, if applicable.

(d) In addition to the completed application form, the following additional documentation shall be affixed and incorporated into the application:

(1) Four color photographs of the structure to be demolished (full views of the front, rear, left, and right sides) labeled with the municipal address of the property and the view shown;

(2) A printed copy of the assessor's aerial map, with the subject property indicated;

