**Sec. 162-1641. Vehicle requirements.**

Each pedicab in operation must meet the following minimum specifications:

(a) Unibody frame construction;

(b) Three wheels;

(c) Rubber on all wheels;

(d) A width of no more than 55 inches and length of no more than ten feet;

(e) Absent of electric or gasoline-powered engine;

(f) Headlights that have a beam of at least 300 feet;

(g) Flashing taillights that are visible from at least 500 feet;

(h) Reflectors on the spokes of all three wheels;

(i) Hydraulic, mechanical disk, or drum brakes that are unaffected by wet conditions;

(j) Body painted with bright, reflective paint.

(M.C.S., Ord. No. 24156, § 1, 10-21-10)

**Sec. 162-1642. Pedicab driver photo identification and uniform.**

All drivers are required to wear a photo identification and uniform clothing containing the operator's name and the affiliated company, of a design specified by the director of the ground transportation bureau of the department of public works. This identification shall be worn during all duty hours and shall be worn to be plainly visible at all times. Every driver must wear a uniform T-shirt, jacket, collared shirt, or sweater with the pedicab company's logo.

(M.C.S., Ord. No. 24156, § 1, 10-21-10; M.C.S., Ord. No. 28624, § 2, 3-11-21)

**Sec. 162-1643. Passenger as controller of cab.**

It shall be unlawful for a pedicab operator to cause a passenger to wait without the passenger's consent or to pick up additional passengers en route without the passenger's consent.

(M.C.S., Ord. No. 24156, § 1, 10-21-10)

**Sec. 162-1644. Number of passengers per vehicle.**

It shall be unlawful to load more passengers in a pedicab than there is seating space available, except that children who are two years of age or younger and held in the arms of an adult may be carried in excess of the above. No pedicab shall be loaded with passengers in such a manner as to obstruct the view of the operator or otherwise interfere with the safe operation of the vehicle.

(M.C.S., Ord. No. 24156, § 1, 10-21-10)

